# PR #1392 review comments — cdz-kernel/src/kernel.rs (v-agent-harness)

Mirrored from https://github.com/camshaft/cadenza/pull/1392 (PR: "[v-agent-harness] 946ab3469").
This PR rewrote the equivalence test per my #1382 point 3 (now compares sync vs async — good).

## 1. `deliver_async` doc says effects are "executed" but timers are *armed*, not executed (Copilot, kernel.rs:335) — doc/contract
> `deliver_async` docs say each effect is "executed" and its result folded back, but timer effects
> are explicitly *armed* by the kernel (no executor call) and later fired via
> `fire_due_timers(_async)`. This wording makes the behavior contract misleading for callers/reducers
> that emit timers.

Valid contract-accuracy point: reword so the doc distinguishes executor-dispatched effects (executed
+ result folded) from timer effects (armed by the kernel, fired later via fire_due_timers).

## 2. Temporaries across `.await` (Copilot, kernel.rs:1506, also :1573) — LIKELY FALSE-POSITIVE, verify
> This test passes references to temporaries across an `.await` (`&SyncAsAsync(StatusReducer)` and
> `&timer_cap()`). Rust will reject this with "temporary value dropped while borrowed" because the
> future holds those references until the await completes. Bind both the async reducer adapter and
> the async authorizer to locals so they live long enough.

⚠ Likely FALSE POSITIVE: the temporaries are passed INLINE into a single
`async_s.deliver_async(…, &SyncAsAsync(StatusReducer), &timer_cap(), …).await.unwrap()` statement —
the temporaries live to the end of that full expression statement, which is exactly when the await
completes, so there's no "dropped while borrowed". EVIDENCE: PR #1392's CI already shows `test
(macos-latest) pass` + `clippy pass` — it compiled. NOT actionable as a compile fix. (Binding to
locals is a fine readability choice, but it's not required and Copilot's "Rust will reject this" is
wrong for the inline-temporary-in-the-awaited-expression case.)
